_Answers and work are shown in the red blocks._

== Butterbeer

Preferences for butter beer can be represented by the following demand curve:

#mitex(`
P_d = 100 - \frac{1}{2} Q_d
`)

The supply curve for butter beer can be represented by the following equation:

#mitex(`
P = 20 + \frac{1}{2} Q
`)

Prices are in galleons and quantity is in bottles.

== Q1 | Total Surplus

What is the market equilibrium price, quantity, and total surplus? Use a graph and algebra to analyze this market.

P\*: \_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_

#block(width: 100%, stroke: (left: 2pt + rgb("#c00000")), inset: (left: 1em))[
  #set text(fill: rgb("#c00000"))

  $60$ galleons.

  From Homework B3, $100 - \frac{1}{2} Q = 20 + \frac{1}{2} Q \rightarrow Q^* = 80$ and $P^* = 60$.
]

Q\*: \_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_

#block(width: 100%, stroke: (left: 2pt + rgb("#c00000")), inset: (left: 1em))[
  #set text(fill: rgb("#c00000"))

  $80$ bottles.
]

TS: \_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_

#block(width: 100%, stroke: (left: 2pt + rgb("#c00000")), inset: (left: 1em))[
  #set text(fill: rgb("#c00000"))

  $3200$ galleons.

  Total surplus is the whole triangle between demand and supply out to $Q^*$, with height the gap between the two intercepts:

  #mitex(`
  \text{TS} = h \cdot b \cdot \tfrac{1}{2} = (100 - 20) \cdot 80 \cdot \tfrac{1}{2} = 3200 \quad (= \text{CS} + \text{PS} = 1600 + 1600)
  `)

  This is the most surplus the market can generate: every bottle up to the eightieth has a buyer whose marginal benefit exceeds a seller's marginal cost, and none beyond it does.
]

== Q2 | Price Ceiling

The Ministry of Magic decided to institute a price ceiling of $50$ galleons to allow many more to afford the treat. Use a graph of the demand curve and the supply curve to evaluate the welfare effects this policy had on the market.

Quantity Supplied: \_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_

#block(width: 100%, stroke: (left: 2pt + rgb("#c00000")), inset: (left: 1em))[
  #set text(fill: rgb("#c00000"))

  $60$ bottles.
]

Quantity Demanded: \_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_

#block(width: 100%, stroke: (left: 2pt + rgb("#c00000")), inset: (left: 1em))[
  #set text(fill: rgb("#c00000"))

  $100$ bottles.
]

Excess, Market Clearing, or Shortage: \_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_

#block(width: 100%, stroke: (left: 2pt + rgb("#c00000")), inset: (left: 1em))[
  #set text(fill: rgb("#c00000"))

  Shortage.
]

CS: \_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_

#block(width: 100%, stroke: (left: 2pt + rgb("#c00000")), inset: (left: 1em))[
  #set text(fill: rgb("#c00000"))

  $2100$ galleons.
]

How much did CS change? \_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_

#block(width: 100%, stroke: (left: 2pt + rgb("#c00000")), inset: (left: 1em))[
  #set text(fill: rgb("#c00000"))

  Up by $500$ galleons.
]

PS: \_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_

#block(width: 100%, stroke: (left: 2pt + rgb("#c00000")), inset: (left: 1em))[
  #set text(fill: rgb("#c00000"))

  $900$ galleons.
]

How much did PS change? \_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_

#block(width: 100%, stroke: (left: 2pt + rgb("#c00000")), inset: (left: 1em))[
  #set text(fill: rgb("#c00000"))

  Down by $700$ galleons.
]

DWL: \_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_

#block(width: 100%, stroke: (left: 2pt + rgb("#c00000")), inset: (left: 1em))[
  #set text(fill: rgb("#c00000"))

  $200$ galleons.
]

#block(width: 100%, stroke: (left: 2pt + rgb("#c00000")), inset: (left: 1em))[
  #set text(fill: rgb("#c00000"))

  #grid(
    columns: (1fr, 1fr),
    gutter: 1em,
    [
      The ceiling of $50$ sits below $P^* = 60$, so it binds. At $50$: $Q_s = 60$ and $Q_d = 100$, a shortage of $40$; only $60$ bottles are exchanged. The buyers who get them value the sixtieth bottle at $P_d = 100 - \frac{1}{2} \cdot 60 = 70$.

      Letter the regions between the curves out to $Q^* = 80$, split at $Q = 60$ and at the prices $70$, $60$, and $50$:

      #mitex(`
      A = (100 - 70) \cdot 60 \cdot \tfrac{1}{2} = 900 \qquad B = (70 - 60) \cdot 60 = 600
      `)

      #mitex(`
      C = (70 - 60) \cdot 20 \cdot \tfrac{1}{2} = 100 \qquad D = (60 - 50) \cdot 60 = 600
      `)

      #mitex(`
      E = (60 - 50) \cdot 20 \cdot \tfrac{1}{2} = 100 \qquad F = (50 - 20) \cdot 60 \cdot \tfrac{1}{2} = 900
      `)
    ],
    [
      #image("data:image/svg+xml;base64,", width: 100%)
    ],
  )

  #align(center, table(
    columns: 4,
    align: (left, center, center, center),
    table.header([], [*CS*], [*PS*], [*DWL*]),
    [Equilibrium], [$A + B + C = 1600$], [$D + E + F = 1600$], [$0$],
    [Ceiling at $50$], [$A + B + D = 2100$], [$F = 900$], [$C + E = 200$],
  ))

  $\Delta \text{CS} = 2100 - 1600 = +500$ and $\Delta \text{PS} = 900 - 1600 = -700$. Region $D$ ($600$) is transferred from sellers to buyers. Regions $C + E$ ($200$) are lost to everyone, because the $20$ bottles between $60$ and $80$ that were worth trading are no longer traded. Total surplus falls from $3200$ to $3000$.
]

== Q3 | Price Floor

The Ministry of Magic decided that the market price wasn't sufficiently high enough for sellers, and decided to institute a price floor to raise the price. Use a graph of the demand curve and the supply curve to evaluate the welfare effects of a price floor of $70$.

